    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d618">

                <pb facs="0740" n="680" sameAs="frus:frus1895p1/pg680"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Olney to Sir Julian Pauncefote</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1895-10-30"><hi rendition="#italic">October 30,
                        1895</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 234.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Excellency</hi>: Adverting to the Department’s note
                    of the 26th instant, concerning the testimony of Capt. C. L. Hooper and Lieut.
                    F. S. Van Boskerck, jr., of the revenue steamer <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Rush</hi>, which seized the <hi rendition="#italic">E. B. Marvin</hi>, I
                    have now the honor to apprise you of the receipt of a letter from the Acting
                    Secretary of the Treasury, of the 28th instant, saying that, in view of the
                    opinion expressed by the United States district attorney at San Francisco, who
                    had conferred with Captain Hooper on the subject that his testimony in the <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Marvin</hi> case was unnecessary, the orders of that
                    officer have been countermanded.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Richard Olney</hi></signed>.</closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
